{{>common_header this title="How to get games" description="How to get PSP games into PPSSPP: dump your own discs, then copy the ISO files to your device over Wi-Fi or USB."}}

<div class="container">
    <h1>How to get games for PPSSPP</h1>

    <h2>What are they?</h2>

    <p>PSP games are games created for the Sony PlayStation Portable, or PSP for short.
        They are normally sold as either small plastic discs or as downloadable content on PSN.
        To use them with PPSSPP, we must turn them into files. An "ISO file" is a raw copy
        of a disc, which can be compressed into a "CSO", or compressed iso (or "CHD", an alternate format).
        For instructions on how to do this yourself, see the
        <a href="/docs/getting-started/dumping-games">instructions on dumping games</a>.
    </p>
    <p>PSP games that have been downloaded from PSN onto a real PSP can be copied off the PSP and played directly.</p>
    <p>There are also free <a href="/gethomebrew">demos and homebrew games</a> you can download.</p>

    <h2>How do I get them on my device?</h2>

    <h3>Any device, over Wi-Fi (easiest)</h3>
    <p>From PPSSPP 1.20, you can upload games from any computer or phone on the same Wi-Fi network:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>On the <b class="inapp">Games</b> tab, go to the folder where you want to put your games.</li>
        <li>Click the Upload icon (a folder with an up arrow).</li>
        <li>Open the address PPSSPP shows in a web browser on the other device, and upload your ISO files there.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Windows/macOS</h3>
    <p>If you are running the Windows or macOS version of PPSSPP and you have the game you want to run as an ISO or CSO file,
        just do File-&gt;Load, or use the <b class="inapp">Games</b> tab to navigate to your game.
        The Up Arrow icon moves up a directory level, and <b class="inapp">Browse...</b> opens your
        system's file browser.</p>

    <h3>Android, over USB</h3>
    <p>If you prefer a cable on Android (or another portable):</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Install PPSSPP on your device</li>
        <li>Connect the device to your PC where you are storing the ISO or CSO file. Android devices can be easily
            connected via USB cable.</li>
        <li>The device should show up in Windows Explorer. Now simply copy over the files to somewhere easy to
            remember - for example, create a folder called "PSP ISO" and copy your ISO and CSO files there.</li>
        <li>Now, start PPSSPP, navigate to your new ISOs on the <b class="inapp">Games</b> tab, then click the game,
            which now should show up with an icon.</li>
        <li>The game should now start.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><a href="/docs/getting-started/installing-games-android#android">More details for Android</a>.</p>

    <h3>iPhone and iPad</h3>
    <p>See the <a href="/getgames_ios">iOS instructions</a>.</p>

